{:edb/db-before-t 1520 :edb/db-after-t 1521 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534833 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:23:56.780Z" 13194139534833 true] [17592186175701 1100 "Find an eigenvector of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}$." 13194139534833 false] [17592186175701 1100 "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/7dad070080562e459c926f5c8fa3d71a56981d68bd3ed4aae768c2f7450685a3.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186175701 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-3 + 4\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4\\textrm{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{-4\\textrm{i}x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 0; - 4x_{1} - 4\\textrm{i}x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534833 false] [17592186175701 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/6786c7bc4d12bc3ee1a335eeb45967475850a585ec36805fe81aa76360d5a729.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/a73682dbe1dfccc14e5b3f0c68aa6e6aa7eb7a67bb475efbbaeaa1e886585554.png)" 13194139534833 true] [17592186175702 1100 "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139534833 true] [17592186175702 1100 "Determine whether $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139534833 false] [17592186175702 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-18 + 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ 24\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}18\\textrm{i}^{2} + 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ 24\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6\\textrm{i}v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139534833 false] [17592186175702 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186175703 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/e6a0d43572790d4daa49659c271c17032f2601458db0f6843257df07c245f372.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/9bf515d20e5a19646680474f0243dba793d1383eade4bf4986c7b11501baa800.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/1afe535b20d0fd1ce03b2b0886424a5ef1a29f4eea7e47c1aa818af67c98e868.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186175703 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}$." 13194139534833 false] [17592186175703 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λ_{1}I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 + 5\\textrm{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 + 5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5\\textrm{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{-5\\textrm{i}x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 0; - 5x_{1} - 5\\textrm{i}x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ \\textrm{i}x_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a complex eigenvalue of $A$, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1\n$$" 13194139534833 false] [17592186175703 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/af8f52f23a6fdcd1f070eff5876415cc884ffaafcbb2fe3f8737c31cc4761c86.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313271 13194139534833 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313306 13194139534833 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313313 13194139534833 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313327 13194139534833 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313334 13194139534833 true] [17592186175853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313264 13194139534833 true] [17592186175853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313285 13194139534833 true] [17592186175853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313292 13194139534833 true] [17592186175853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313299 13194139534833 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313257 13194139534833 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313278 13194139534833 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313320 13194139534833 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313341 13194139534833 true] [17592186176393 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534833 true] [17592186176393 1100 "Determine whether $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139534833 false] [17592186176393 1100 "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139534833 true] [17592186176393 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ 6-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ -6{\\text{i}}^{2}-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=3\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3\\text{i}.$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186176396 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534833 true] [17592186176396 1100 "Given that $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{a_{1}a_{2}}{b_{1}b_{2}}$?" 13194139534833 false] [17592186176396 1100 "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186176396 1145 "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313257 1017 #edb/ref 17592186313258 13194139534833 true] [17592186313257 1089 "q-140387" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313257 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534833 true] [17592186313257 1100 "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313257 1113 #uuid "98eb69e4-fc5d-5211-9182-b032cfbab6b4" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313257 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5-5\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 10 & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-5-5\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}+(5-5\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+5\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1-5\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}{(-2)(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313259 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313260 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313261 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313262 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313263 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1041 "selection" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1104 #edb/ref 17592186313259 13194139534833 true] [17592186313258 1110 #uuid "deb31aa7-cb8b-575a-b421-246f08ecb24d" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313259 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313259 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534833 true] [17592186313259 1111 #uuid "c1525b61-1791-537e-a7c9-ed078a59c211" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313260 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313260 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534833 true] [17592186313260 1111 #uuid "dd1e9fdf-66ef-5e8f-8c7d-5bec55e8637d" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313261 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313261 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534833 true] [17592186313261 1111 #uuid "a6548834-4797-5767-916e-b73981f8869b" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313262 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313262 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534833 true] [17592186313262 1111 #uuid "59255893-b8cf-5491-82ad-a934f5ad64b5" 13194139534833 true] [17592186313263 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139